38
00:03:14,018 --> 00:03:17,256
So let's talk about the interaction tax.

39
00:03:18,144 --> 00:03:22,839
in most web applications, when the
user interact, do something, press a

40
00:03:22,839 --> 00:03:28,363
button, mark a checkbox, then there
is an HTTP request sent to the server.

41
00:03:28,865 --> 00:03:33,071
And then on the server, there is some
sort of interaction with the database,

42
00:03:33,650 --> 00:03:38,710
and eventually a reply is returned
back to the client, and the client

43
00:03:38,755 --> 00:03:41,681
render the result of that interaction.

44
00:03:42,210 --> 00:03:50,389
So what we actually get is repeatedly
a request response between the client

45
00:03:50,414 --> 00:03:56,520
and the server whenever the user want to
filter data, navigate, query, and so on.

46
00:03:56,993 --> 00:04:03,668
And this is a tax most users
on most web applications pay.

47
00:04:04,876 --> 00:04:08,923
So the question is: Do
we have an alternative?

48
00:04:10,585 --> 00:04:12,336
Can we do it a bit different?

49
00:04:13,054 --> 00:04:22,917
And the answer is yes We can
use data that arrives to the web

50
00:04:22,921 --> 00:04:27,959
browser and perform the query, the
filtering, and so on, on that data.

51
00:04:27,980 --> 00:04:33,235
So this way, we avoid the back-and-forward
interaction with the server.

52
00:04:34,616 --> 00:04:40,533
Now, what I'm going to introduce
and explain is not an architecture

53
00:04:40,533 --> 00:04:48,942
you should take and apply in full,
on the entire web application.

54
00:04:49,549 --> 00:04:55,820
you should use your judgment,
and if you find this architecture

55
00:04:55,831 --> 00:05:01,635
suitable, then do it carefully
and implement it when it fits.

56
00:05:01,688 --> 00:05:06,086
It can be a small part of the
entire, web application you develop.

57
00:05:06,090 --> 00:05:09,012
It doesn't have to be the
entire web application.

58
00:05:10,680 --> 00:05:17,028
So So this technique, of course,
fits when dealing with reading

59
00:05:17,035 --> 00:05:19,822
data, not writing data, of course.

60
00:05:20,316 --> 00:05:25,859
So moving the reading to the web
browser, we still need to remember

61
00:05:25,881 --> 00:05:31,738
that we should have on the server
side, in most cases, a database that is

62
00:05:31,786 --> 00:05:35,406
actually the source of the, the data,
the source, the only source of truth.

63
00:05:36,045 --> 00:05:43,396
And then, the server side can generate
a JSON document with that data that we

64
00:05:43,396 --> 00:05:49,315
want the web browser to hold so that, the
query will be performed using that data.

65
00:05:49,315 --> 00:05:54,255
So we can have a server side that
return, JSON with all data, and that

66
00:05:54,263 --> 00:06:00,223
data will be in the web browser, which
will allow the web browser to, query

67
00:06:00,255 --> 00:06:04,403
and filter and do everything the user
wants, or most of the operations,

68
00:06:04,942 --> 00:06:07,512
without, interacting with the server.

69
00:06:07,542 --> 00:06:09,175
Do it all locally.

70
00:06:10,619 --> 00:06:17,516
So we won't need the server
round trip for every local read

71
00:06:18,516 --> 00:06:24,879
So I want to introduce you with, one of
the ventures I'm working on, which is

72
00:06:24,880 --> 00:06:29,193
a ca- the case study for implementing
this architecture because it fits.

73
00:06:30,368 --> 00:06:33,338
And I'm talking about Nana.Events.

74
00:06:34,126 --> 00:06:37,161
Now, if you visit Nana.Events

75
00:06:37,172 --> 00:06:38,678
website, this is what you get.

76
00:06:39,150 --> 00:06:45,285
It's a platform that, fetch data from
various resources and eventually,

77
00:06:45,676 --> 00:06:50,091
present you with information about,
conferences in tech and business.

78
00:06:50,976 --> 00:06:58,526
And, you can perform various queries,
and when you do that, you can see

79
00:06:58,527 --> 00:07:01,425
that, the reaction is immediate.

80
00:07:01,562 --> 00:07:07,217
You don't need to wait till, the
server will complete a query,

81
00:07:07,699 --> 00:07:10,203
and return back, data, and so on.

82
00:07:10,231 --> 00:07:11,821
Everything is very fast.

83
00:07:11,840 --> 00:07:20,277
Now, you can even click here on World
Map and get, map that allows you to see

84
00:07:20,280 --> 00:07:27,503
the conferences, in various, countries,
and you can specify the duration that

85
00:07:27,529 --> 00:07:32,806
you find interesting, and you can
easily change that, and that will

86
00:07:32,806 --> 00:07:35,176
affect, of course, on the data we get.

87
00:07:35,845 --> 00:07:37,878
And you can press and get information.

88
00:07:37,961 --> 00:07:43,965
And everything happens local in the
web browser without back and forward

89
00:07:43,996 --> 00:07:45,902
interaction with the server side.

90
00:07:46,205 --> 00:07:52,416
Now, this case study fits very well
to this architecture because we're

91
00:07:52,418 --> 00:07:54,679
not dealing with huge amount of data.

92
00:07:55,480 --> 00:07:59,088
As you can see, I show how
many events, currently, we can

93
00:07:59,088 --> 00:08:02,001
expect, looking into the future.

94
00:08:02,452 --> 00:08:08,824
and this number, of course, goes down
and goes up because time pass and,

95
00:08:08,903 --> 00:08:11,060
events take place, so then they take…

96
00:08:11,154 --> 00:08:13,124
are taken away from this, counter.

97
00:08:14,402 --> 00:08:22,731
So In that specific platform, you can see
that most of the operations are reading

98
00:08:22,774 --> 00:08:25,701
operation, getting data, not writing data.

99
00:08:25,900 --> 00:08:30,096
the user can submit, a new event.

100
00:08:30,152 --> 00:08:33,763
There is a button, Add Event,
over here on the bottom, and it

101
00:08:33,764 --> 00:08:38,630
can add new events, that he wants
to be-- to add to this platform.

102
00:08:38,682 --> 00:08:43,366
but that platform also works without
