105
00:08:53,719 --> 00:08:58,369
there is a crawler that takes that
data and add it to this platform

106
00:08:59,369 --> 00:09:07,291
Now, in addition to the fact that the
amount of data is not too big, That data

107
00:09:07,322 --> 00:09:14,789
is not, data that if it is not the most
accurate data, then it means a disaster.

108
00:09:14,823 --> 00:09:20,057
if there is a user that use this
platform and for some reason, he gets

109
00:09:20,094 --> 00:09:24,413
to use, he find himself using, an
older version of that JSON document

110
00:09:24,425 --> 00:09:27,679
with old data, it's not a disaster.

111
00:09:27,815 --> 00:09:33,500
So this is also a characteristic of
those cases where this architecture fits.

112
00:09:34,815 --> 00:09:37,791
So bottom line, when
you visit Nana.Events,

113
00:09:37,807 --> 00:09:41,334
most of the reading operations are
performed, on the front end, on

114
00:09:41,334 --> 00:09:45,783
the, in the web browser, or it can
also be a, an mobile application for

115
00:09:45,838 --> 00:09:49,400
mobile telephone and so on, because
you can easily take every website

116
00:09:49,478 --> 00:09:51,394
and wrap it as an application.

117
00:09:52,852 --> 00:09:55,959
And the user experience is great.

118
00:09:57,717 --> 00:10:03,426
Now, let's talk a bit more about the
specific architecture of Nana.Events.

119
00:10:03,437 --> 00:10:06,549
So on the server side, we have
RESTful web services implemented in

120
00:10:06,551 --> 00:10:09,649
PHP and MySQL for holding the data.

121
00:10:10,641 --> 00:10:12,168
And then the client side.

122
00:10:12,168 --> 00:10:16,513
The client side is a single page
application, SPA, implemented

123
00:10:16,525 --> 00:10:17,746
in vanilla JavaScript.

124
00:10:17,804 --> 00:10:24,195
I chose vanilla JavaScript because I
prefer, the smallest amount of code

125
00:10:24,303 --> 00:10:29,387
possible, on the front end so that
the user experience will be better.

126
00:10:29,411 --> 00:10:31,010
I didn't want, big files.

127
00:10:31,366 --> 00:10:36,919
there are techniques to achieve that and
still use those libraries we are familiar

128
00:10:36,919 --> 00:10:41,272
with, but this specific case is so simple.

129
00:10:41,286 --> 00:10:46,554
So I picked the, the vanilla JavaScript
approach and avoided the complexity

130
00:10:46,554 --> 00:10:51,044
involved when tech-- implementing
various techniques, server side

131
00:10:51,045 --> 00:10:53,789
rendering, and so on to keep it simple.

132
00:10:53,899 --> 00:10:56,531
It is important because we
have, the long run where we

133
00:10:56,531 --> 00:10:58,251
need to maintain the platform.

134
00:10:58,251 --> 00:10:59,649
It's not just about developing it.

135
00:11:00,337 --> 00:11:04,137
And it's more about the features
that are added continuously and

136
00:11:04,137 --> 00:11:06,427
the bugs that are-- we need to fix.

137
00:11:07,677 --> 00:11:11,643
So what we get specifically
with Nana.Events

138
00:11:11,661 --> 00:11:16,847
is that the data is acquired, using
humans that submit events and using

139
00:11:16,847 --> 00:11:21,546
crawler that get the data from
legit resources, such as python.org,

140
00:11:21,559 --> 00:11:22,690
that list the events.

141
00:11:24,210 --> 00:11:29,045
And then, every, submitted event,
whether it was by the crawler or the

142
00:11:29,046 --> 00:11:33,961
human, there is, a process of validating
that this is a legit event, and there

143
00:11:33,961 --> 00:11:40,381
is an AI agent doing that work on the
server side, and then the data is added

144
00:11:40,381 --> 00:11:44,500
to the database, which is the single
source of truth of this platform.

145
00:11:45,230 --> 00:11:50,627
And then whenever the user visit
the website there is, JSON waiting

146
00:11:50,630 --> 00:11:52,551
for him with, the up-to-date data.

147
00:11:52,587 --> 00:11:57,208
Now that JSON is updated
whenever the database is updated.

148
00:11:57,583 --> 00:12:03,176
So bottom line, what the user gets
is a JSON document that travel

149
00:12:03,198 --> 00:12:05,021
to the web browser with all data.

150
00:12:05,066 --> 00:12:09,283
Now remember, we're not dealing with a
huge amount of data, so for that reason,

151
00:12:09,769 --> 00:12:18,110
this architecture fits great So in order
to improve the user experience, I started

152
00:12:18,301 --> 00:12:24,039
thinking about cases in which the user
doesn't have, fast internet connection.

153
00:12:24,049 --> 00:12:28,432
Maybe he's using a w-- a mobile telephone
and the internet connection is not good.

154
00:12:28,905 --> 00:12:33,480
So for that reason, I decided
to have two JSON documents.

155
00:12:33,499 --> 00:12:34,936
There is the tiny.json

156
00:12:34,950 --> 00:12:40,081
document that includes just the
information needed, to show it

157
00:12:40,112 --> 00:12:43,464
on screen when user visit the
website for the first time.

158
00:12:44,774 --> 00:12:49,978
so when user visit the website for the
first time, first, there is the tiny.json

159
00:12:49,994 --> 00:12:56,369
that travels to the client with the
data that is required to show something.

160
00:12:56,934 --> 00:13:01,659
By the time the user understand
how to use, the website, what are

161
00:13:01,660 --> 00:13:04,230
the possibilities, the events.json,

162
00:13:04,247 --> 00:13:09,276
the big JSON file, arrives, and then we
have all data inside the web browser.

163
00:13:10,279 --> 00:13:15,440
Now, to improve the performance, you
can imagine that the front end, the

164
00:13:15,442 --> 00:13:19,840
code in JavaScript we have inside the
web browser, also takes the data and

165
00:13:19,844 --> 00:13:25,418
store it in the local storage so that
next time it will be available for fast

166
00:13:25,435 --> 00:13:30,568
rendering without waiting Now, when
we talk about zero latency, we should

167
00:13:30,570 --> 00:13:36,651
pay attention to the fact that what
we have here is not absolutely zero.

168
00:13:36,651 --> 00:13:39,093
It's almost, but not zero latency.

169
00:13:39,358 --> 00:13:45,910
when dealing with the network round trip
for every, filtering, querying the data,

170
00:13:46,711 --> 00:13:50,151
then we have zero latency when it comes…

171
00:13:50,199 --> 00:13:54,278
Because we don't have network
round trips whenever the user

172
00:13:54,279 --> 00:13:55,908
filter or query the data.

173
00:13:56,217 --> 00:14:01,190
But we still have some latency,
with, downloading the file,

174
00:14:01,528 --> 00:14:03,538
executing the code in JavaScript.

175
00:14:03,573 --> 00:14:05,302
everything is on the main thread.

176
00:14:05,835 --> 00:14:10,453
And of sco- of course, when user adds
an event, then this is another story.

177
00:14:10,715 --> 00:14:13,618
We do have network latency in that case.

178
00:14:13,636 --> 00:14:17,477
But the bottom line, if you think
about the entire picture, the

179
00:14:17,477 --> 00:14:21,350
user experience, and I have just
demonstrated, i- is amazing.

180
00:14:21,783 --> 00:14:26,523
It can filter data, get what you want.

181
00:14:26,576 --> 00:14:32,912
you can say, just online events
and, or just, hybrid events and

182
00:14:32,921 --> 00:14:37,473
just events in, let's say in the Go.

183
00:14:38,742 --> 00:14:42,265
Oh, so Go, there is nothing,
with the limitations we gave.

184
00:14:42,695 --> 00:14:45,264
Let's say, not Go, but, Java.

185
00:14:45,306 --> 00:14:46,395
Okay, we have something.

186
00:14:46,947 --> 00:14:52,568
And we can always, of course, go
to world map, and then I can just,

187
00:14:52,619 --> 00:14:59,672
specify the duration and get to see the
events, according to this time limits.

188
00:15:00,836 --> 00:15:02,917
And I can just,

189
00:15:02,971 --> 00:15:06,100
press and get more information,
specifically for that event.

190
00:15:06,288 --> 00:15:10,460
And this is very convenient because,
for me, when I travel to speak in a

191
00:15:10,463 --> 00:15:14,527
conference, I can always check whether
there is another conference I can

192
00:15:14,795 --> 00:15:17,824
attend at the same time and, having…

193
00:15:17,878 --> 00:15:22,641
and eventually have more than one
conference in this using the same trip

194
00:15:23,641 --> 00:15:28,259
Now, the trade-off, between, using
this architecture and avoiding it.

195
00:15:28,705 --> 00:15:35,516
So it fits, mainly when we deal
with, public datasets, not too big,

196
00:15:36,371 --> 00:15:41,325
and when most of the, interaction
of the user is about reading

197
00:15:41,327 --> 00:15:43,304
data, filtering data, and so on.

198
00:15:44,126 --> 00:15:51,311
And also when the accuracy of the data,
is not that important, so we can tolerate,

199
00:15:51,346 --> 00:15:54,305
data that is not the most up-to-date data.

200
00:15:55,614 --> 00:16:01,059
but if we're dealing with a huge
amount of data or data that, is

201
00:16:01,059 --> 00:16:03,756
not public and has private i-i…

202
00:16:03,905 --> 00:16:09,246
data items, or when there is a need
that all clients will see the exact

203
00:16:09,249 --> 00:16:15,381
same data, then this architecture,
won't fit, or let's say poor fit.

204
00:16:16,075 --> 00:16:20,355
And you should think about
those, characteristics when

205
00:16:20,355 --> 00:16:25,024
you consider implementing the
architecture I introduced.

206
00:16:26,051 --> 00:16:33,453
So the takeaway, if you find this
architecture, fits for your case, do

207
00:16:33,453 --> 00:16:36,157
it intentionally, do it on purpose.

208
00:16:36,187 --> 00:16:44,044
Think about it in advance and, think
about how to allow the user interface

209
00:16:44,097 --> 00:16:49,811
getting, the data that is going
to query and explore on the client

210
00:16:49,812 --> 00:16:54,988
side, bundled together, in a simple
way, such as a big JSON document.

211
00:16:54,999 --> 00:16:57,437
Don't make it difficult
for the client side.

212
00:16:58,027 --> 00:17:02,785
The second thing is think about cases
where the internet connection is

213
00:17:02,785 --> 00:17:07,486
not strong enough, so maybe we want
to give good experience for those

214
00:17:07,495 --> 00:17:10,804
that don't have internet, strong
internet, fast internet connection.

215
00:17:11,036 --> 00:17:15,535
So a smaller version of the JSON
document that arrives first and

216
00:17:15,537 --> 00:17:21,000
allows the client side to render
something just to keep the user happy.

217
00:17:22,114 --> 00:17:28,713
And the third thing is, Don't
forget that there are still, lots

218
00:17:28,713 --> 00:17:34,319
of, activities on the web browser,
JavaScript running in order to allow

219
00:17:34,345 --> 00:17:37,483
the filtering, querying, and so on.

220
00:17:37,861 --> 00:17:40,369
And for that, we need computing resources.

221
00:17:40,369 --> 00:17:44,238
So depends on what you develop.

222
00:17:44,360 --> 00:17:50,761
Maybe the price is too big, and
for that reason, the architecture

223
00:17:50,796 --> 00:17:53,302
